1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,282
The stator of our reference motor is not a
solid block of steel.

2
00:00:04,322 --> 00:00:10,282
It's a stack of sheets, each 0.35 mm
thick, insulated from its neighbours.

3
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:13,632
Why make it from steel, and why cut the
sheets so thin?

4
00:00:13,672 --> 00:00:16,055
Because iron does two things air doesn't:

5
00:00:16,095 --> 00:00:19,196
it saturates, and it turns energy into
heat.

6
00:00:20,096 --> 00:00:22,389
First, last lesson's predictions.

7
00:00:22,429 --> 00:00:27,016
One: a 3 mm magnet with Br = 1.2 T (μrec =
1.05),

8
00:00:27,056 --> 00:00:31,094
across a 0.5 mm gap, gives about 1.02 T.

9
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:37,088
Two: doubling the magnet gives only about
1.10 T; the gap can't exceed Br.

10
00:00:37,128 --> 00:00:42,916
Three: 40 mm of iron at μr = 4000 equals
0.01 mm of air;

11
00:00:42,956 --> 00:00:47,275
at μr = 40, a full 1 mm, twice the gap.

12
00:00:47,315 --> 00:00:50,875
Real iron moves between those cases.
Here's why.

13
00:00:51,775 --> 00:00:55,145
In iron, electron spins carry magnetic
moments that line

14
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:58,612
up with their neighbours, in regions
called domains.

15
00:00:58,652 --> 00:01:02,953
In demagnetised iron, the domains point
different ways and cancel.

16
00:01:02,993 --> 00:01:07,001
Apply a field H, and the walls move:
domains along H grow.

17
00:01:07,041 --> 00:01:14,100
That gives a magnetisation M thousands of
times larger than H, and B = μ₀(H + M).

18
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:17,562
When nearly every moment points along the
field,

19
00:01:17,602 --> 00:01:22,645
M stops growing: saturation. From then on,
B rises only as μ₀H.

20
00:01:23,545 --> 00:01:27,442
Here's that behaviour for our motor's
steel class:

21
00:01:27,482 --> 00:01:30,656
a 0.35 mm non-oriented silicon steel.

22
00:01:30,696 --> 00:01:33,645
Dots are typical datasheet values at 50
Hz;

23
00:01:33,685 --> 00:01:37,491
the curve is a smooth fit that later
lessons reuse.

24
00:01:37,531 --> 00:01:42,937
Up to 1.2 tesla, under 200 amps per metre
will do; then the curve bends.

25
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:49,001
1.5 tesla needs 1700 amps per metre, and
1.8 tesla over 11000.

26
00:01:49,041 --> 00:01:54,192
That bend, near 1.5 tesla, is the
saturation knee.

27
00:01:54,232 --> 00:01:56,676
Typical values: cut edges and mechanical

28
00:01:56,716 --> 00:02:00,073
stress shift a real part's curve to the
right.

29
00:02:00,973 --> 00:02:05,007
Divide B by μ₀H, and you get the relative
permeability, μr.

30
00:02:05,047 --> 00:02:10,492
For our steel, it peaks near 7700, at
about 0.8 tesla,

31
00:02:10,532 --> 00:02:15,973
falls to about 700 at 1.5, and to about
130 at 1.8.

32
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:19,302
For small changes, the slope dB/dH
matters:

33
00:02:19,342 --> 00:02:24,401
the incremental permeability. At 1.5 tesla
it's only about 50.

34
00:02:24,441 --> 00:02:27,566
So our constant 4000 is a fair round
number

35
00:02:27,606 --> 00:02:31,418
below about 1.3 tesla, and badly wrong
above.

36
00:02:32,318 --> 00:02:38,715
Put this steel into Lesson 3's circuit: 40
mm of iron, a 0.5 mm gap, and a coil.

37
00:02:38,755 --> 00:02:43,269
The ampere-turns divide: the iron needs H
times its length l,

38
00:02:43,309 --> 00:02:45,584
and the gap needs B g / μ₀.

39
00:02:45,624 --> 00:02:49,930
The iron takes 1 % at one tesla, 10 % at
1.5,

40
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:55,253
and almost 40 % at 1.8. The gap no longer
dominates.

41
00:02:55,293 --> 00:03:01,772
Teeth make it worse. Our teeth are 6.5 mm
wide on a 10.7 mm pitch,

42
00:03:01,812 --> 00:03:06,616
so they carry 1.65 times the gap flux
density.

43
00:03:06,656 --> 00:03:10,844
With Lesson 3's magnet, such iron runs at
1.6 tesla,

44
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:16,668
takes over a quarter of the MMF, and the
gap falls to 0.97 tesla.

45
00:03:17,568 --> 00:03:21,243
Saturation is half the story. Drive the
steel back and forth,

46
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:23,431
and B doesn't retrace its path.

47
00:03:23,471 --> 00:03:25,636
Coming down from 1.5 tesla,

48
00:03:25,676 --> 00:03:31,000
this model keeps about 0.7 tesla at zero
field: the remanence.

49
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,697
A reverse field of about 40 amps per
metre,

50
00:03:33,737 --> 00:03:36,631
the coercive field, brings B back to zero.

51
00:03:36,671 --> 00:03:41,438
It's computed with the Jiles–Atherton
model: domain-wall pinning on an ideal,

52
00:03:41,478 --> 00:03:44,984
hysteresis-free curve, tuned to this
steel's losses.

53
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:47,297
Lower peaks trace smaller, nested loops,

54
00:03:47,337 --> 00:03:50,440
though this simple model draws them too
wide.

55
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:55,701
Wind N turns on a steel ring of cross-
section A and mean path length l.

56
00:03:55,741 --> 00:03:59,977
Faraday's law gives the voltage, N A
dB/dt;

57
00:04:00,017 --> 00:04:04,254
Ampère's law gives the current, H l / N.

58
00:04:04,294 --> 00:04:09,323
So the power is the volume A l, times H
dB/dt. Over one cycle,

59
00:04:09,363 --> 00:04:14,693
the energy per unit volume is ∮H dB: the
area inside the loop.

60
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:18,642
At 1.5 tesla that's about 290 joules per
cubic metre,

61
00:04:18,682 --> 00:04:24,827
turned to heat each cycle, so hysteresis
loss grows in proportion to frequency.

62
00:04:24,867 --> 00:04:29,416
In 1892 Steinmetz fitted this loss as B to
the 1.6;

63
00:04:29,456 --> 00:04:34,995
for our steel the fitted exponent rises
from 1.6 to 2.3.

64
00:04:35,895 --> 00:04:39,452
Iron also conducts. A changing flux
induces an electric

65
00:04:39,492 --> 00:04:43,284
field that drives currents in the iron:
eddy currents.

66
00:04:43,324 --> 00:04:46,377
Solid iron lets them circle the whole
cross-section;

67
00:04:46,417 --> 00:04:49,354
insulated sheets confine each to a thin
loop.

68
00:04:49,394 --> 00:04:56,910
In a thin sheet, the current density grows
linearly from the mid-plane.

69
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:06,043
Averaged over the sheet and a cycle, the
loss per unit volume is π²t²f²B̂² over 6ρ.

70
00:05:06,083 --> 00:05:14,251
For 0.35 mm sheets at 50 Hz and 1.5 tesla,
that's 0.28 watts per kilogram.

71
00:05:14,291 --> 00:05:17,410
That assumes the flux fills the sheet,

72
00:05:17,450 --> 00:05:24,185
so t must be well below the skin depth:
about 2 mm at 50 Hz, 0.7 mm at 400.

73
00:05:25,085 --> 00:05:28,046
Datasheets give total loss at several
frequencies;

74
00:05:28,086 --> 00:05:30,507
a common reading splits it in three.

75
00:05:30,547 --> 00:05:35,148
Hysteresis, in f; classical eddy loss, in
f², from our formula;

76
00:05:35,188 --> 00:05:41,488
and an excess term in f to the 1.5, from
eddy currents around moving domain walls.

77
00:05:41,528 --> 00:05:46,378
Fitted from 50 to 400 Hz, it stays within
6 % of the data.

78
00:05:46,418 --> 00:05:51,989
Hysteresis dominates at 50 Hz; at 400,
eddy currents take over.

79
00:05:52,029 --> 00:05:55,360
At 1 kHz the skin effect makes it over-
predict.

80
00:05:55,400 --> 00:06:00,708
And narrow cut strips of this grade have
measured about 40 % above the datasheet.

81
00:06:01,608 --> 00:06:05,829
Each pole pair passing a tooth is one
electrical

82
00:06:05,869 --> 00:06:10,922
cycle of the stator flux: f = p n / 60,
with n in rpm.

83
00:06:10,962 --> 00:06:15,381
At 3000 rpm, with four pole pairs, that's
200 Hz.

84
00:06:15,421 --> 00:06:20,811
There, at 1.5 tesla, this steel loses
about 15 watts per kilogram.

85
00:06:20,851 --> 00:06:27,611
With 0.43 kg of stator steel, that's about
6 watts: the copper loss at 10 amps.

86
00:06:27,651 --> 00:06:31,769
A rough estimate: parts of the stator run
below 1.5 tesla,

87
00:06:31,809 --> 00:06:34,721
but harmonics and cut edges add loss.

88
00:06:34,761 --> 00:06:36,865
The rotor iron turns with the field,

89
00:06:36,905 --> 00:06:40,323
so it sees nearly steady flux, and far
less loss.

90
00:06:41,223 --> 00:06:45,404
Trace a loop yourself: stack 28 rings of
this steel,

91
00:06:45,444 --> 00:06:50,737
50 by 40 mm, with a 200-turn primary and a
100-turn secondary.

92
00:06:50,777 --> 00:06:54,721
Drive the primary at 50 Hz through a 1 Ω
sense resistor;

93
00:06:54,761 --> 00:06:57,156
its voltage, on X, measures H.

94
00:06:57,196 --> 00:07:03,714
The secondary voltage follows dB/dt, so
integrate it with 100 kΩ and 1 µF:

95
00:07:03,754 --> 00:07:07,647
that voltage, on Y, is proportional to B.

96
00:07:07,687 --> 00:07:12,616
For 1.5 tesla, the primary needs about 4.6
volts peak.

97
00:07:12,656 --> 00:07:17,700
Expect about 1.2 volts on X, and 73
millivolts on Y.

98
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:20,177
At 50 Hz, eddy currents widen the loop;

99
00:07:20,217 --> 00:07:24,338
at lower frequency it shrinks toward the
hysteresis loop.

100
00:07:25,238 --> 00:07:27,418
Three predictions before next time.

101
00:07:27,458 --> 00:07:32,191
One: cut the lamination thickness in half,
same frequency and flux density.

102
00:07:32,231 --> 00:07:35,785
What happens to the classical eddy-current
loss?

103
00:07:36,485 --> 00:07:39,401
Two: double the frequency, same flux
density.

104
00:07:39,441 --> 00:07:43,948
By what factors do hysteresis and eddy-
current losses change?

105
00:07:44,648 --> 00:07:46,768
Three: our motor has 8 poles.

106
00:07:46,808 --> 00:07:52,000
What's the electrical frequency in its
stator iron at 5000 rpm?

107
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:57,785
Next time: the field the rotor's magnets
put into the air gap, slot by slot.
